Charge syndrome
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Coloboma, Choanal atresia, Ear anomalies/deafness, Retardation of growth/development

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-009
Reason for Visit: Acute respiratory distress and choking secondary to severe swallowing difficulties.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the child experienced a sudden choking episode during a fluid feed, leading to audible stridor, increased work of breathing, and transient perioral cyanosis. Assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the immediate, life-threatening risk of complete airway obstruction or aspiration pneumonia requiring urgent clinical clearance and airway assessment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: He choked on his drink, turned blue around the lips, and now his breathing is very noisy and labored.
History of Present Illness: The parents report a 2-hour history of acute respiratory distress following a choking event. The patient has a known history of cranial nerve palsies frequently causing severe swallowing difficulties. During a routine liquid feed, the patient coughed violently and exhibited transient cyanosis. The patient has a history of repaired choanal atresia and a tracheoesophageal fistula, making airway management complex. He also has a coloboma of the iris and profound sensorineural ear anomalies/deafness, limiting his ability to communicate distress beyond crying. Retardation of physical growth and global developmental delay are noted.
Functional Status: Dependent on parents for all feeding and mobility; utilizes modified feeding techniques.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme parental anxiety regarding recurrent aspiration and airway compromise.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/60 mmHg | HR: 130 bpm | Temp: 37.2 C | RR: 32 bpm | SpO2: 93% | Wt: 12.0 Kg
General: Child is alert but anxious, exhibiting mild intercostal retractions and an audible upper airway transmitted sound (stridor/gurgling).
Physical Exam: Facial exam reveals a distinct coloboma of the right iris and abnormally shaped pinnae. Neurological exam confirms cranial nerve deficits affecting the gag reflex and palate elevation. Retardation of growth/development is evident based on low weight for age. Respiratory exam shows tachypnea and transmitted upper airway rhonchi, but lung bases are clear to auscultation without focal fine crackles. Cardiovascular exam reveals a healed sternotomy scar (previous heart defect repair); heart sounds are dual with a grade 2/6 systolic murmur.
Environmental Observations: Suction machine is present in the room but was not turned on during the event.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular respiratory medications; thickener powder available but allegedly under-dosed today.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Charge syndrome (with acute micro-aspiration and upper airway secretions) - Status: Acute complication
Differentials:

Lower Respiratory Tract Aspiration Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely (currently) due to the absence of fever, clear lung bases, and the immediate onset of symptoms pointing to upper airway pooling rather than established lung consolidation.

Viral Croup - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of a prodromal viral illness, absence of a barking cough, and clear temporal relationship to feeding.

Tracheomalacia Collapse - Ruled out/unlikely as the stridor is inspiratory and wet, resolving partially with coughing, rather than a fixed expiratory wheeze.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of choking, cyanosis, and increased work of breathing in the context of severe cranial nerve palsies demanded urgent assessment. The objective findings of tachypnea, border-line oxygen saturation (93%), and pooled upper airway secretions confirmed a micro-aspiration event with impending airway compromise. This assessment could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period because the immediate risk of total airway occlusion or rapid decompensation required in-person auscultation, airway clearing, and strict reassessment of feeding protocols to stabilize the patient at home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed gentle oropharyngeal suctioning to clear pooled secretions, which immediately improved the stridor and reduced the respiratory rate.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you strictly measure the fluid thickener with a standardized scoop, the liquid stays out of his airway; therefore, adopting this exact measurement routine prevents future terrifying choking episodes. When you operate the suction machine daily for a test run, it is ready instantly; building this equipment check into your morning routine guarantees rapid response.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the pediatric speech pathology team to reassess fluid consistency.
DME/Safety (Optional): Ensured the suction machine battery is fully charged and appropriately sized catheters are bedside.
Follow-up: GP review tomorrow morning to assess for delayed onset of aspiration pneumonia.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Call 000 immediately if the child turns blue again, if the chest is sucking in deeply with every breath, or if he becomes lethargic and difficult to wake.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Aspiration pneumonia can develop 24-48 hours after a choking event; current clearance does not rule out future infection.
Clinical Course: The upper airway is now clear, but the cranial nerve deficit remains. Strict adherence to thickened fluids is mandatory to prevent recurrence.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek urgent medical review if the child develops a fever over 38.0 C, a new wet cough, or if breathing becomes rapid and shallow over the next two days.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The suctioning removed the fluid trapped above his vocal cords that was causing the noisy breathing. Proper thickener makes the fluid move slowly so his weakened nerves can coordinate the swallow.
Actionable Steps: Strictly measure the prescribed thickener. Keep the patient nil-by-mouth for thin liquids. Keep the suction machine turned on and ready during all feeds.

